{"schema_version":1,"merge_readiness":"advisory","lenses":[{"name":"correctness","score":8,"ran":true,"findings":[{"file":"plugins/agent/impact/plugin.sh","category":"logic","severity":"low","line":426,"message":"The fallback `${_rc_disp:-interrupted}` fires only when `router_reason_disposition` returns empty — which only happens when `_rc_reason` is empty — but `_rc_reason` is always non-empty when `router_rc != 0`, making the fallback unreachable; if it ever fired, `interrupted` would be a semantically wrong disposition for an unknown failure reason (the catch-all from `router_reason_disposition` is `unavailable`, not `interrupted`); the same wrong fallback appears again on line 431 in the printf fallback path.","introduced":true},{"file":"plugins/agent/impact/plugin.sh","category":"logic","severity":"low","line":621,"message":"The else-branch of the v2 merge writes `impact_json` unchanged — with no `result_contract`, `disposition`, or `reason` fields — if jq fails on `. + {result_contract:2,...}`; in practice jq will not fail because `_impact_envelope_schema_ok` already validated the JSON, but unlike every other fallback write in this file (which spells out a full v2 `printf` literal), this else-branch silently produces a v1-only result that breaks the v2 contract guarantee; a matching v2 `printf` fallback like the one at line 430 would close the gap.","introduced":true},{"file":"plugins/agent/impact/plugin.sh","category":"logic","severity":"low","line":433,"message":"At every inline write site (lines 180, 433, 473, 560, 583, 623) `_IMPACT_RESULT_WRITTEN=1` is set unconditionally after the `||` fallback `printf`, even when both the jq+atomic_write and the printf fallback both failed (e.g. disk-full); the `_impact_write_result` helper is stricter — it sets the flag only inside the `if _json=... && atomic_write ...` success branch; if both writes fail at an inline site, the signal handler's guard reads `_IMPACT_RESULT_WRITTEN=1` and skips writing the interrupted result, leaving no result file on disk at all.","introduced":true}]},{"name":"performance","score":8,"ran":true,"findings":[{"file":"plugins/agent/impact/plugin.sh","category":"unnecessary I/O","severity":"low","line":87,"message":"_impact_run_entry calls _impact_input three times sequentially, each forking a new jq process and re-reading the same ZBUILD_STAGE_INPUTS file; a single jq invocation extracting all three fields would cut the file-open and process-fork cost to one-third.","introduced":true},{"file":"plugins/agent/impact/plugin.sh","category":"hot-path inefficiency","severity":"low","line":609,"message":"The success path now runs three sequential jq subprocesses over the same $impact_json string (one pre-existing .verdict extract, one new .missing|length for _missing_count, one new merge for _v2_impact) where a single jq invocation could extract verdict, missing length, and produce the merged object in one pass; absolute cost is small given the 600 s model-call budget but the redundancy is introduced by this change.","introduced":true}]},{"name":"red-team","score":8,"ran":true,"findings":[{"file":"plugins/agent/impact/plugin.sh","category":"logic","severity":"medium","line":617,"message":"The v2 merge on the success path has a silent fallback (else branch, line 621) that writes the raw LLM impact_json without result_contract, disposition, or reason when jq fails; _IMPACT_RESULT_WRITTEN=1 is set either way so no second write opportunity exists, meaning a jq failure silently produces a v1-shaped artifact from a manifest that declares result_contract:2 — the engine would apply v1 control flow to what was intended as a v2 result.","introduced":true},{"file":"plugins/agent/impact/plugin.sh","category":"logic","severity":"low","line":426,"message":"The disposition default ':-interrupted' (line 426 and 431) is used when router_reason_disposition returns empty; the current implementation never returns empty for a non-empty reason (has a catch-all), but if router_reason_disposition is extended and gains a new reason it does not recognise, or if the sourcing of router-rc-classify.sh fails silently (no set -e in the sourced library context), all unclassified router failures would default to disposition=interrupted (retryable) rather than the correct halting disposition, making a config or infra error look like a transient retry.","introduced":true},{"file":"plugins/agent/impact/plugin.sh","category":"logic","severity":"low","line":416,"message":"When _router_rc_classify returns _rc_verdict='fail' (rc=2 router_config_error, or any unrecognised rc → router_rc_nonzero), the condition '_rc_verdict == error' is false so _out_verdict becomes 'incomplete'; the v2 disposition (misconfigured or unavailable) does cause the engine to halt correctly, but the verdict and disposition are semantically contradictory — a cycle orchestrator that reads verdict independently of disposition would interpret 'incomplete' as re-iterate; this behaviour was pre-existing in the v1 code and is not worsened, but the v2 migration did not correct it.","introduced":false},{"file":"tests/unit/impact-no-path-construction-test.sh","category":"coverage","severity":"low","line":28,"message":"SPEC-8's grep patterns match bare-variable forms (artifacts_dir/design.md, $state_dir/...) but do not match brace-quoted forms (${artifacts_dir}/design.md), leaving a gap already noted by the spec-correspondence stage; a future refactor that introduces brace-quoted path constructions would pass the test while violating the SPEC.","introduced":true}]},{"name":"scope","score":9,"ran":true,"findings":[{"file":"plugins/agent/impact/plugin.sh","category":"scope","severity":"low","line":null,"message":"_impact_run_entry retains a v1 calling-convention remnant — `local state_file=\"${2:-}\"` and `_fallback_dir=\"$(dirname \"${state_file:-.}\")/artifacts\"` — to derive a write target when ZBUILD_ARTIFACT_DIR is absent; this is a path construction from the state_file argument inside a function that is otherwise v2-only, and is not caught by SPEC-8's grep patterns (which target input-path literals); the issue's 'no artifact paths constructed in code' principle is not violated by the letter of SPEC-8 but the spirit leaks through the error branch; this change introduced the pattern (it did not exist before this PR).","introduced":true}]},{"name":"security","score":8,"ran":true,"findings":[{"file":"plugins/agent/impact/plugin.sh","category":"injection","severity":"low","line":null,"message":"Fallback printf statements constructing JSON (e.g., the router-failure and empty-response paths) substitute _rc_reason and _rc_disp via %s without JSON-escaping; if the router RC classifier ever returns a reason string containing '\"' or '\\', the fallback produces malformed JSON — this pattern is pre-existing (v1 used the same printf fallback) and the change extends it to new exit paths.","introduced":false},{"file":"plugins/agent/impact/plugin.sh","category":"logic","severity":"low","line":null,"message":"The new early-exit paths in _impact_run_entry write verdict:'broken', a value not declared in manifest.yaml's valid_verdicts:[complete,incomplete,error]; per issue #1708 the engine treats any verdict outside the declared set as a structural failure, so these paths would produce structural failures rather than validated-contract failures — this appears intentional (modelled after the plan plugin) but is not recorded in valid_verdicts nor in the manifest comment.","introduced":true},{"file":"tests/integration/impact-router-timeout-782-test.sh","category":"injection","severity":"low","line":null,"message":"New SPEC-9 test setup constructs the ZBUILD_STAGE_INPUTS JSON via printf with bare %s substitution of $SCOPE_MANIFEST, $DESIGN_MD, and $PLAN_JSON paths; if a temp path contains '\"' or '\\' (e.g., on a runner with unusual TMPDIR), the JSON is malformed and the test silently resolves wrong paths rather than failing explicitly.","introduced":true},{"file":"tests/integration/impact-envelope-recovery-test.sh","category":"injection","severity":"low","line":null,"message":"Same printf-into-JSON pattern used for the new v2 fixture setup (STATE_DIR and ARTIFACTS_DIR substituted via %s without escaping); pre-existing test fixtures use the same pattern, but this instance is newly introduced by the v2 migration.","introduced":true},{"file":"plugins/agent/impact/plugin.sh","category":"logic","severity":"low","line":null,"message":"_impact_on_signal references $STAGE_SIGNAL_DISPOSITION and $STAGE_SIGNAL_REASON without explicit :-empty guards; under set -u, if stage_signal_begin fires the callback before those variables are exported by the signal library, the handler aborts without writing the interrupted result, defeating the signal-guard contract.","introduced":true}]}],"findings":[{"file":"plugins/agent/impact/plugin.sh","category":"hot-path inefficiency","line":609,"severity":"low","lenses":["performance"],"messages":["The success path now runs three sequential jq subprocesses over the same $impact_json string (one pre-existing .verdict extract, one new .missing|length for _missing_count, one new merge for _v2_impact) where a single jq invocation could extract verdict, missing length, and produce the merged object in one pass; absolute cost is small given the 600 s model-call budget but the redundancy is introduced by this change."]},{"file":"plugins/agent/impact/plugin.sh","category":"logic","line":null,"severity":"low","lenses":["security"],"messages":["The new early-exit paths in _impact_run_entry write verdict:'broken', a value not declared in manifest.yaml's valid_verdicts:[complete,incomplete,error]; per issue #1708 the engine treats any verdict outside the declared set as a structural failure, so these paths would produce structural failures rather than validated-contract failures — this appears intentional (modelled after the plan plugin) but is not recorded in valid_verdicts nor in the manifest comment.","_impact_on_signal references $STAGE_SIGNAL_DISPOSITION and $STAGE_SIGNAL_REASON without explicit :-empty guards; under set -u, if stage_signal_begin fires the callback before those variables are exported by the signal library, the handler aborts without writing the interrupted result, defeating the signal-guard contract."]},{"file":"plugins/agent/impact/plugin.sh","category":"logic","line":426,"severity":"low","lenses":["correctness","red-team"],"messages":["The disposition default ':-interrupted' (line 426 and 431) is used when router_reason_disposition returns empty; the current implementation never returns empty for a non-empty reason (has a catch-all), but if router_reason_disposition is extended and gains a new reason it does not recognise, or if the sourcing of router-rc-classify.sh fails silently (no set -e in the sourced library context), all unclassified router failures would default to disposition=interrupted (retryable) rather than the correct halting disposition, making a config or infra error look like a transient retry.","The fallback `${_rc_disp:-interrupted}` fires only when `router_reason_disposition` returns empty — which only happens when `_rc_reason` is empty — but `_rc_reason` is always non-empty when `router_rc != 0`, making the fallback unreachable; if it ever fired, `interrupted` would be a semantically wrong disposition for an unknown failure reason (the catch-all from `router_reason_disposition` is `unavailable`, not `interrupted`); the same wrong fallback appears again on line 431 in the printf fallback path."]},{"file":"plugins/agent/impact/plugin.sh","category":"logic","line":433,"severity":"low","lenses":["correctness"],"messages":["At every inline write site (lines 180, 433, 473, 560, 583, 623) `_IMPACT_RESULT_WRITTEN=1` is set unconditionally after the `||` fallback `printf`, even when both the jq+atomic_write and the printf fallback both failed (e.g. disk-full); the `_impact_write_result` helper is stricter — it sets the flag only inside the `if _json=... && atomic_write ...` success branch; if both writes fail at an inline site, the signal handler's guard reads `_IMPACT_RESULT_WRITTEN=1` and skips writing the interrupted result, leaving no result file on disk at all."]},{"file":"plugins/agent/impact/plugin.sh","category":"logic","line":617,"severity":"medium","lenses":["red-team"],"messages":["The v2 merge on the success path has a silent fallback (else branch, line 621) that writes the raw LLM impact_json without result_contract, disposition, or reason when jq fails; _IMPACT_RESULT_WRITTEN=1 is set either way so no second write opportunity exists, meaning a jq failure silently produces a v1-shaped artifact from a manifest that declares result_contract:2 — the engine would apply v1 control flow to what was intended as a v2 result."]},{"file":"plugins/agent/impact/plugin.sh","category":"logic","line":621,"severity":"low","lenses":["correctness"],"messages":["The else-branch of the v2 merge writes `impact_json` unchanged — with no `result_contract`, `disposition`, or `reason` fields — if jq fails on `. + {result_contract:2,...}`; in practice jq will not fail because `_impact_envelope_schema_ok` already validated the JSON, but unlike every other fallback write in this file (which spells out a full v2 `printf` literal), this else-branch silently produces a v1-only result that breaks the v2 contract guarantee; a matching v2 `printf` fallback like the one at line 430 would close the gap."]},{"file":"plugins/agent/impact/plugin.sh","category":"scope","line":null,"severity":"low","lenses":["scope"],"messages":["_impact_run_entry retains a v1 calling-convention remnant — `local state_file=\"${2:-}\"` and `_fallback_dir=\"$(dirname \"${state_file:-.}\")/artifacts\"` — to derive a write target when ZBUILD_ARTIFACT_DIR is absent; this is a path construction from the state_file argument inside a function that is otherwise v2-only, and is not caught by SPEC-8's grep patterns (which target input-path literals); the issue's 'no artifact paths constructed in code' principle is not violated by the letter of SPEC-8 but the spirit leaks through the error branch; this change introduced the pattern (it did not exist before this PR)."]},{"file":"plugins/agent/impact/plugin.sh","category":"unnecessary I/O","line":87,"severity":"low","lenses":["performance"],"messages":["_impact_run_entry calls _impact_input three times sequentially, each forking a new jq process and re-reading the same ZBUILD_STAGE_INPUTS file; a single jq invocation extracting all three fields would cut the file-open and process-fork cost to one-third."]},{"file":"tests/integration/impact-envelope-recovery-test.sh","category":"injection","line":null,"severity":"low","lenses":["security"],"messages":["Same printf-into-JSON pattern used for the new v2 fixture setup (STATE_DIR and ARTIFACTS_DIR substituted via %s without escaping); pre-existing test fixtures use the same pattern, but this instance is newly introduced by the v2 migration."]},{"file":"tests/integration/impact-router-timeout-782-test.sh","category":"injection","line":null,"severity":"low","lenses":["security"],"messages":["New SPEC-9 test setup constructs the ZBUILD_STAGE_INPUTS JSON via printf with bare %s substitution of $SCOPE_MANIFEST, $DESIGN_MD, and $PLAN_JSON paths; if a temp path contains '\"' or '\\' (e.g., on a runner with unusual TMPDIR), the JSON is malformed and the test silently resolves wrong paths rather than failing explicitly."]},{"file":"tests/unit/impact-no-path-construction-test.sh","category":"coverage","line":28,"severity":"low","lenses":["red-team"],"messages":["SPEC-8's grep patterns match bare-variable forms (artifacts_dir/design.md, $state_dir/...) but do not match brace-quoted forms (${artifacts_dir}/design.md), leaving a gap already noted by the spec-correspondence stage; a future refactor that introduces brace-quoted path constructions would pass the test while violating the SPEC."]}],"pre_existing":[{"file":"plugins/agent/impact/plugin.sh","category":"injection","line":null,"severity":"low","lenses":["security"],"messages":["Fallback printf statements constructing JSON (e.g., the router-failure and empty-response paths) substitute _rc_reason and _rc_disp via %s without JSON-escaping; if the router RC classifier ever returns a reason string containing '\"' or '\\', the fallback produces malformed JSON — this pattern is pre-existing (v1 used the same printf fallback) and the change extends it to new exit paths."]},{"file":"plugins/agent/impact/plugin.sh","category":"logic","line":416,"severity":"low","lenses":["red-team"],"messages":["When _router_rc_classify returns _rc_verdict='fail' (rc=2 router_config_error, or any unrecognised rc → router_rc_nonzero), the condition '_rc_verdict == error' is false so _out_verdict becomes 'incomplete'; the v2 disposition (misconfigured or unavailable) does cause the engine to halt correctly, but the verdict and disposition are semantically contradictory — a cycle orchestrator that reads verdict independently of disposition would interpret 'incomplete' as re-iterate; this behaviour was pre-existing in the v1 code and is not worsened, but the v2 migration did not correct it."]}],"summary":"11 merge-readiness finding(s) across 5 lens(es) (0 critical, 0 high).","escalation_note":null}
